# THE FLOORED SIGN LAW, REALIZED — and every document it was cited from opens
# somewhere else or nowhere.
#
# This file, `examples/table/div/sign.id` and `examples/cbackend/modsign.id`
# each settled `%` by citing `docs/rulings.md` § "Modulo and floor division".
# `git log --all --diff-filter=A -- docs/rulings.md` IS EMPTY — never added on
# any branch — and thirteen tracked files still name it, six of them compiler
# sources. The two citations under it are no better: `§12 B-4`, which these
# files carried before, is defined by no document in either tree, and `law §62`
# — `src/comptime.zig`'s authority for "modulo sign … fully defined" and
# `src/dnir_lower.zig`'s for the zero-divisor guard — OPENS ON A DIFFERENT
# SUBJECT: `docs/spec/law.md` has seventeen numbered sections and no §62 at all,
# and the constitution's §62 is EVIDENCE MUST BE PRIVATE TO THE RUN. Nothing
# that opens carries this law; `docs/spec/numerics.md` held the sign rows once
# and is now a projection page without them (55f8f120). So the standing
# instruction is `examples/table/tailcall.id`'s — cite a document that opens, or
# state the measurement. This file states the measurement.
#
# AND `%` WAS ALL THREE FILES MEASURED. The ruling settles `//` in the same
# sentence, and `//` carries its own tag so that it can — `binopTagOf`: "`//`
# KEEPS ITS OWN IDENTITY … the two operators became one tag and the backend then
# emitted `sdiv` for both" — with its own correction in `emitFlooredDivRem` and
# in the wasm `.idiv` arm. NO FIXTURE IN THIS CORPUS REACHED IT. It is the four
# leading columns here.
#
# `gate/divisor.sh` IS NOT DUPLICATED HERE. It owns this law from the compiler
# side and states it more strongly than any fixture can — §3 its own sign matrix
# over operands read from the environment, §4 the folder required to agree, §1
# the zero-divisor fault. It also measures NOTHING on this host: it reads its
# faults from three EXECUTED direct binaries, so `direct_native_absent` exits it
# 2 before §3, §4 and §5, none of which need a faulting binary or the direct
# backend. That is its STILL OWED, not this file's.
#
# THE OPERANDS ARE PARAMETERS, so no divisor sign is known at any site and no row
# can be served by a fold: this file measures the REALIZER.
# `examples/table/div/sign.id` is the same law through literal operands and
# `evalNumeric`, and the four cells the two files share make fold-and-realize
# agreement a measured fact rather than a reading of two switch statements. That
# is worth measuring because `evalNumeric` held BOTH laws at once — `native_fold`
# answering `@divTrunc`/`@rem` and the ordinary branch `@divFloor`/`@mod`, twelve
# lines apart.
#
# EACH COLUMN CONVICTS, measured by mutating this file and re-running:
#
#   `%` divisor negated       -1 -1 1 1     the remainder takes the DIVISOR's
#                                           sign, which is the whole ruling
#   `%` replaced by `//`      3 -4 -4 3     the two halves collapse
#   `//` as `0-((0-a)//b)`    4 -3 -3 4     a ceiling round convicts
#   `//` replaced by `/`      REFUSED, `return type mismatch: expected 'i64',
#                             got 'f64'` — which is why `/`'s columns live in
#                             the sibling. Inside a relation declared `: i64`
#                             the open row cannot be SPELLED, so `GAP-065`'s
#                             typing row stands in the way of its own
#                             measurement. It is still open; half a numeric law
#                             is worse than none.
#
# WHAT IS NOT A CONTROL HERE, measured rather than assumed:
# `IDOL_FLOOR_FIXUP_ALWAYS=1` and `IDOL_DIVZERO_GUARD_ALWAYS=1` leave this
# program's artifact BYTE-IDENTICAL — both only reach a division whose divisor is
# already proved non-negative or already unguarded, and a parameter is neither.
# The env-varied cache is not the reason: `IDOL_WASM_TAILCALL=1` on
# `examples/table/tailcall.id` moves the bytes through the same compiler.
#
# Measured by `gate/divsign.sh`, the first harness to compare an `# expect:`
# line in this family against a run.
# expect: 3 -4 -4 3 1 1 -1 -1
# @corpus current
quotient: i64 = (a: i64, b: i64)
  a // b

remainder: i64 = (a: i64, b: i64)
  a % b

main: i64 = ()
  print("{quotient(7, 2)} {quotient(-7, 2)} {quotient(7, -2)} {quotient(-7, -2)} {remainder(7, 2)} {remainder(-7, 2)} {remainder(7, -2)} {remainder(-7, -2)}")
  0
